\title{Discrete structures and induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{How much structure can a discrete model induce, and which choices
must already be supplied? A finite graph makes this question concrete:
its incidence data specify how vertex values differ along edges, while
weights are further choices. The [finite incidence presentation](fdip-0002)
and [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) make the carrier and weights
explicit. Choosing these data, changing coordinates and eliminating fields
are different operations. Reordering vertices changes a representation;
changing weights changes the model. [Eliminating fields](fdip-0018)
additionally requires an action and a measure.}

\p{Induced geometry provides one motivation. Shamir's dynamical random
spatial lattice yields an effective metric conditional on the existence
of suitable localized low-energy states
\citet{Abstract}{shamir1994dynamical}. This motivates separating supplied
microscopic data, state assumptions and induced structure. The broader
[conjecture](fdip-003X) asks for an arithmetic scheme with a Clifford
sheaf and finite-group action, a moduli-controlled continuum and background
comparison, and quantum-field-theory parameters given by special L-values.
These are targets:
the finite constructions do not derive spacetime, physical parameters,
Standard Model equations or gravitational equations.}

\p{Fields and operator products lead through pairings, parity and
differences to elimination over a ring. States and reference measures
then make real Gaussian expectations, covariance and constrained laws
precise. Gaussian elimination and formal interactions give retained
observables; symmetry, sectors and Clifford constructions organize their
background comparisons before the arithmetic examples. The closing
[synthesis](fdip-003W) relates these results to the conjecture and its
remaining interfaces. The four-cycle recurs throughout, connecting each
new structure to specified fields, weights and observables.}

